1
00:00:00,833 --> 00:00:02,265
CHAPTER XIX.

2
00:00:03,399 --> 00:00:10,265
THE next day opened a new scene at Longbourn. Mr. Collins made his declaration in form. Having

3
00:00:10,265 --> 00:00:15,600
resolved to do it without loss of time, as his leave of absence extended only to the following

4
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:20,799
Saturday, and having no feelings of diffidence to make it distressing to himself even at the moment,

5
00:00:20,799 --> 00:00:25,733
he set about it in a very orderly manner, with all the observances which he supposed

6
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:30,665
a regular part of the business. On finding Mrs. Bennet, Elizabeth, and one of the younger

7
00:00:30,665 --> 00:00:34,566
girls together, soon after breakfast, he addressed the mother in these words,—

8
00:00:35,399 --> 00:00:40,365
“May I hope, madam, for your interest with your fair daughter Elizabeth,

9
00:00:40,365 --> 00:00:45,299
when I solicit for the honour of a private audience with her in the course of this morning?”

10
00:00:46,132 --> 00:00:51,765
Before Elizabeth had time for anything but a blush of surprise, Mrs. Bennet instantly answered,—

11
00:00:51,765 --> 00:00:58,665
“Oh dear! Yes, certainly. I am sure Lizzy will be very happy—I am sure she can have no

12
00:00:58,665 --> 00:01:04,299
objection. Come, Kitty, I want you upstairs.” And gathering her work together, she was

13
00:01:04,299 --> 00:01:09,765
hastening away, when Elizabeth called out,— “Dear ma’am, do not go. I beg you will not

14
00:01:09,765 --> 00:01:14,566
go. Mr. Collins must excuse me. He can have nothing to say to me that anybody

15
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:20,566
need not hear. I am going away myself.” “No, no, nonsense, Lizzy. I desire you

16
00:01:20,566 --> 00:01:25,433
will stay where you are.” And upon Elizabeth’s seeming really, with vexed and embarrassed looks,

17
00:01:25,433 --> 00:01:32,032
about to escape, she added, “Lizzy, I insist upon your staying and hearing Mr. Collins.”

18
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:38,700
Elizabeth would not oppose such an injunction; and a moment’s consideration making her also sensible

19
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:43,600
that it would be wisest to get it over as soon and as quietly as possible, she sat down again,

20
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:49,132
and tried to conceal, by incessant employment, the feelings which were divided between distress

21
00:01:49,132 --> 00:01:55,733
and diversion. Mrs. Bennet and Kitty walked off, and as soon as they were gone, Mr. Collins began,—

22
00:01:56,933 --> 00:02:04,000
“Believe me, my dear Miss Elizabeth, that your modesty, so far from doing you any disservice,

23
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,799
rather adds to your other perfections. You would have been less amiable in my eyes had there not

24
00:02:10,799 --> 00:02:16,365
been this little unwillingness; but allow me to assure you that I have your respected mother’s

25
00:02:16,365 --> 00:02:22,000
permission for this address. You can hardly doubt the purport of my discourse, however

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,433
your natural delicacy may lead you to dissemble; my attentions have been too marked to be mistaken.

27
00:02:28,433 --> 00:02:35,765
Almost as soon as I entered the house I singled you out as the companion of my future life.

28
00:02:35,765 --> 00:02:42,066
But before I am run away with by my feelings on this subject, perhaps it will be advisable for me

29
00:02:42,066 --> 00:02:47,333
to state my reasons for marrying—and, moreover, for coming into Hertfordshire with the design

30
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:54,365
of selecting a wife, as I certainly did.” The idea of Mr. Collins, with all his solemn

31
00:02:54,365 --> 00:03:00,000
composure, being run away with by his feelings, made Elizabeth so near laughing that she could

32
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,966
not use the short pause he allowed in any attempt to stop him farther, and he continued,—

33
00:03:05,799 --> 00:03:12,100
“My reasons for marrying are, first, that I think it a right thing for every clergyman in

34
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:16,733
easy circumstances (like myself) to set the example of matrimony in his parish;

35
00:03:17,265 --> 00:03:24,100
secondly, that I am convinced it will add very greatly to my happiness; and,

36
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:30,600
thirdly, which perhaps I ought to have mentioned earlier, that it is the particular advice and

37
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:37,265
recommendation of the very noble lady whom I have the honour of calling patroness. Twice has she

38
00:03:37,265 --> 00:03:44,032
condescended to give me her opinion (unasked too!) on this subject; and it was but the very Saturday

39
00:03:44,032 --> 00:03:49,665
night before I left Hunsford,—between our pools at quadrille, while Mrs. Jenkinson was arranging

40
00:03:49,665 --> 00:03:56,865
Miss De Bourgh’s footstool,—that she said, ‘Mr. Collins, you must marry. A clergyman like you must

41
00:03:56,865 --> 00:04:03,532
marry. Choose properly, choose a gentlewoman for my sake, and for your own; let her be an active,

42
00:04:03,532 --> 00:04:09,966
useful sort of person, not brought up high, but able to make a small income go a good way. This

43
00:04:09,966 --> 00:04:16,966
is my advice. Find such a woman as soon as you can, bring her to Hunsford, and I will visit her.’

44
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:23,333
Allow me, by the way, to observe, my fair cousin, that I do not reckon the notice and kindness of

45
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:29,265
Lady Catherine de Bourgh as among the least of the advantages in my power to offer. You will find her

46
00:04:29,265 --> 00:04:35,799
manners beyond anything I can describe; and your wit and vivacity, I think, must be acceptable

47
00:04:35,799 --> 00:04:42,165
to her, especially when tempered with the silence and respect which her rank will inevitably excite.

48
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:48,466
Thus much for my general intention in favour of matrimony; it remains to be told why my

49
00:04:48,466 --> 00:04:53,466
views were directed to Longbourn instead of my own neighbourhood, where I assure you there are many

50
00:04:53,466 --> 00:05:00,165
amiable young women. But the fact is, that being, as I am, to inherit this estate after the death

51
00:05:00,165 --> 00:05:05,865
of your honoured father (who, however, may live many years longer), I could not satisfy myself

52
00:05:05,865 --> 00:05:11,500
without resolving to choose a wife from among his daughters, that the loss to them might be as

53
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:16,865
little as possible when the melancholy event takes place—which, however, as I have already said,

54
00:05:16,865 --> 00:05:23,500
may not be for several years. This has been my motive, my fair cousin, and I flatter myself

55
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:29,865
it will not sink me in your esteem. And now nothing remains for me but to assure you in

56
00:05:29,865 --> 00:05:36,000
the most animated language of the violence of my affection. To fortune I am perfectly indifferent,

57
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,365
and shall make no demand of that nature on your father, since I am well aware that it could not

58
00:05:40,365 --> 00:05:45,299
be complied with; and that one thousand pounds in the 4 per cents., which will not be yours

59
00:05:45,299 --> 00:05:49,966
till after your mother’s decease, is all that you may ever be entitled to. On that head,

60
00:05:49,966 --> 00:05:55,899
therefore, I shall be uniformly silent: and you may assure yourself that no ungenerous reproach

61
00:05:55,899 --> 00:06:03,233
shall ever pass my lips when we are married.” It was absolutely necessary to interrupt him now.

62
00:06:03,833 --> 00:06:10,865
“You are too hasty, sir,” she cried. “You forget that I have made no answer. Let me

63
00:06:10,865 --> 00:06:15,665
do it without further loss of time. Accept my thanks for the compliment you are paying

64
00:06:15,665 --> 00:06:19,500
me. I am very sensible of the honour of your proposals, but it is impossible

65
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:26,200
for me to do otherwise than decline them.” “I am not now to learn,” replied Mr. Collins,

66
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:31,066
with a formal wave of the hand, “that it is usual with young ladies to reject the

67
00:06:31,066 --> 00:06:36,233
addresses of the man whom they secretly mean to accept, when he first applies for their favour;

68
00:06:36,233 --> 00:06:43,600
and that sometimes the refusal is repeated a second or even a third time. I am, therefore, by

69
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:49,132
no means discouraged by what you have just said, and shall hope to lead you to the altar ere long.”

70
00:06:50,399 --> 00:06:55,865
“Upon my word, sir,” cried Elizabeth, “your hope is rather an extraordinary one after

71
00:06:55,865 --> 00:07:01,899
my declaration. I do assure you that I am not one of those young ladies (if such young ladies there

72
00:07:01,899 --> 00:07:07,365
are) who are so daring as to risk their happiness on the chance of being asked a second time. I am

73
00:07:07,365 --> 00:07:13,000
perfectly serious in my refusal. You could not make me happy, and I am convinced that I am the

74
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,865
last woman in the world who would make you so. Nay, were your friend Lady Catherine to know me,

75
00:07:17,865 --> 00:07:22,299
I am persuaded she would find me in every respect ill qualified for the situation.”

76
00:07:22,299 --> 00:07:30,399
“Were it certain that Lady Catherine would think so,” said Mr. Collins, very gravely—“but I cannot

77
00:07:30,399 --> 00:07:35,333
imagine that her Ladyship would at all disapprove of you. And you may be certain that when I

78
00:07:35,333 --> 00:07:40,066
have the honour of seeing her again I shall speak in the highest terms of your modesty,

79
00:07:40,066 --> 00:07:46,433
economy, and other amiable qualifications.” “Indeed, Mr. Collins, all praise of me will

80
00:07:46,433 --> 00:07:52,365
be unnecessary. You must give me leave to judge for myself, and pay me the compliment of believing

81
00:07:52,365 --> 00:07:59,200
what I say. I wish you very happy and very rich, and by refusing your hand, do all in my power to

82
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:04,200
prevent your being otherwise. In making me the offer, you must have satisfied the delicacy of

83
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:08,700
your feelings with regard to my family, and may take possession of Longbourn estate whenever it

84
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:15,532
falls, without any self-reproach. This matter may be considered, therefore, as finally settled.” And

85
00:08:15,532 --> 00:08:20,933
rising as she thus spoke, she would have quitted the room, had not Mr. Collins thus addressed her,—

86
00:08:21,665 --> 00:08:25,865
“When I do myself the honour of speaking to you next on the subject,

87
00:08:25,865 --> 00:08:31,566
I shall hope to receive a more favourable answer than you have now given me; though I am far from

88
00:08:31,566 --> 00:08:35,865
accusing you of cruelty at present, because I know it to be the established custom of your

89
00:08:35,865 --> 00:08:40,733
sex to reject a man on the first application, and, perhaps, you have even now said as much to

90
00:08:40,733 --> 00:08:45,399
encourage my suit as would be consistent with the true delicacy of the female character.”

91
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:52,299
“Really, Mr. Collins,” cried Elizabeth, with some warmth, “you puzzle me exceedingly. If

92
00:08:52,299 --> 00:08:55,265
what I have hitherto said can appear to you in the form of encouragement,

93
00:08:55,265 --> 00:08:59,933
I know not how to express my refusal in such a way as may convince you of its being one.”

94
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:06,399
“You must give me leave to flatter myself, my dear cousin, that your refusal of my addresses are

95
00:09:06,399 --> 00:09:12,765
merely words of course. My reasons for believing it are briefly these:—It does not appear to me

96
00:09:12,765 --> 00:09:18,165
that my hand is unworthy your acceptance, or that the establishment I can offer would be any other

97
00:09:18,165 --> 00:09:23,566
than highly desirable. My situation in life, my connections with the family of De Bourgh,

98
00:09:23,566 --> 00:09:27,466
and my relationship to your own, are circumstances highly in my favour;

99
00:09:27,466 --> 00:09:33,365
and you should take it into further consideration that, in spite of your manifold attractions,

100
00:09:33,365 --> 00:09:39,165
it is by no means certain that another offer of marriage may ever be made you. Your portion is

101
00:09:39,165 --> 00:09:43,966
unhappily so small, that it will in all likelihood undo the effects of your loveliness and amiable

102
00:09:43,966 --> 00:09:49,200
qualifications. As I must, therefore, conclude that you are not serious in your rejection of me,

103
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:54,700
I shall choose to attribute it to your wish of increasing my love by suspense, according

104
00:09:54,700 --> 00:10:01,966
to the usual practice of elegant females.” “I do assure you, sir, that I have no pretensions

105
00:10:01,966 --> 00:10:07,733
whatever to that kind of elegance which consists in tormenting a respectable man. I would rather be

106
00:10:07,733 --> 00:10:13,299
paid the compliment of being believed sincere. I thank you again and again for the honour you

107
00:10:13,299 --> 00:10:19,265
have done me in your proposals, but to accept them is absolutely impossible. My feelings in

108
00:10:19,265 --> 00:10:26,265
every respect forbid it. Can I speak plainer? Do not consider me now as an elegant female

109
00:10:26,265 --> 00:10:31,799
intending to plague you, but as a rational creature speaking the truth from her heart.”

110
00:10:32,566 --> 00:10:38,500
“You are uniformly charming!” cried he, with an air of awkward gallantry;

111
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:45,466
“and I am persuaded that, when sanctioned by the express authority of both your excellent parents,

112
00:10:45,466 --> 00:10:52,299
my proposals will not fail of being acceptable.” To such perseverance in wilful self-deception

113
00:10:52,299 --> 00:10:58,000
Elizabeth would make no reply, and immediately and in silence withdrew; determined, that if he

114
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,765
persisted in considering her repeated refusals as flattering encouragement, to apply to her father,

115
00:11:02,765 --> 00:11:07,299
whose negative might be uttered in such a manner as must be decisive, and whose

116
00:11:07,299 --> 00:11:12,399
behaviour at least could not be mistaken for the affectation and coquetry of an elegant female.

117
00:11:14,865 --> 00:11:15,899
CHAPTER XX.

118
00:11:22,966 --> 00:11:28,200
MR. COLLINS was not left long to the silent contemplation of his successful love; for Mrs.

119
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:32,933
Bennet, having dawdled about in the vestibule to watch for the end of the conference, no sooner saw

120
00:11:32,933 --> 00:11:37,665
Elizabeth open the door and with quick step pass her towards the staircase, than she entered the

121
00:11:37,665 --> 00:11:42,833
breakfast-room, and congratulated both him and herself in warm terms on the happy prospect of

122
00:11:42,833 --> 00:11:48,100
their nearer connection. Mr. Collins received and returned these felicitations with equal pleasure,

123
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:52,365
and then proceeded to relate the particulars of their interview, with the result of which

124
00:11:52,365 --> 00:11:57,233
he trusted he had every reason to be satisfied, since the refusal which his cousin had steadfastly

125
00:11:57,233 --> 00:12:02,399
given him would naturally flow from her bashful modesty and the genuine delicacy of her character.

126
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:08,200
This information, however, startled Mrs. Bennet: she would have been glad to be equally satisfied

127
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:12,899
that her daughter had meant to encourage him by protesting against his proposals, but she dared

128
00:12:12,899 --> 00:12:19,500
not believe it, and could not help saying so. “But depend upon it, Mr. Collins,” she added,

129
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:25,066
“that Lizzy shall be brought to reason. I will speak to her about it myself directly. She is a

130
00:12:25,066 --> 00:12:30,100
very headstrong, foolish girl, and does not know her own interest; but I will make her know it.”

131
00:12:30,833 --> 00:12:38,032
“Pardon me for interrupting you, madam,” cried Mr. Collins; “but if she is really headstrong and

132
00:12:38,032 --> 00:12:43,132
foolish, I know not whether she would altogether be a very desirable wife to a man in my situation,

133
00:12:43,132 --> 00:12:48,532
who naturally looks for happiness in the marriage state. If, therefore, she actually

134
00:12:48,532 --> 00:12:53,799
persists in rejecting my suit, perhaps it were better not to force her into accepting me,

135
00:12:53,799 --> 00:12:59,500
because, if liable to such defects of temper, she could not contribute much to my felicity.”

136
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:06,100
“Sir, you quite misunderstand me,” said Mrs. Bennet, alarmed. “Lizzy is only headstrong

137
00:13:06,100 --> 00:13:11,165
in such matters as these. In everything else she is as good-natured a girl as ever lived. I

138
00:13:11,165 --> 00:13:15,066
will go directly to Mr. Bennet, and we shall very soon settle it with her, I am sure.”

139
00:13:15,765 --> 00:13:19,500
She would not give him time to reply, but hurrying instantly to her husband,

140
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:24,665
called out, as she entered the library,— “Oh, Mr. Bennet, you are wanted immediately;

141
00:13:24,665 --> 00:13:30,233
we are all in an uproar. You must come and make Lizzy marry Mr. Collins, for she vows she will

142
00:13:30,233 --> 00:13:34,066
not have him; and if you do not make haste he will change his mind and not have her.”

143
00:13:34,066 --> 00:13:39,000
Mr. Bennet raised his eyes from his book as she entered, and fixed them on her face

144
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,132
with a calm unconcern, which was not in the least altered by her communication.

145
00:13:43,966 --> 00:13:48,000
“I have not the pleasure of understanding you,” said he, when she had finished

146
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,233
her speech. “Of what are you talking?” “Of Mr. Collins and Lizzy. Lizzy declares

147
00:13:54,233 --> 00:13:58,000
she will not have Mr. Collins, and Mr. Collins begins to say that he will not have Lizzy.”

148
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:01,899
“And what am I to do on the occasion? It seems a hopeless business.”

149
00:14:02,799 --> 00:14:07,433
“Speak to Lizzy about it yourself. Tell her that you insist upon her marrying him.”

150
00:14:08,032 --> 00:14:11,032
“Let her be called down. She shall hear my opinion.”

151
00:14:11,799 --> 00:14:15,600
Mrs. Bennet rang the bell, and Miss Elizabeth was summoned to the library.

152
00:14:16,433 --> 00:14:21,165
“Come here, child,” cried her father as she appeared. “I have sent for you on an affair

153
00:14:21,165 --> 00:14:26,100
of importance. I understand that Mr. Collins has made you an offer of marriage. Is it true?”

154
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:31,066
Elizabeth replied that it was. “Very well—and this offer of marriage

155
00:14:31,066 --> 00:14:33,365
you have refused?” “I have, sir.”

156
00:14:33,899 --> 00:14:39,299
“Very well. We now come to the point. Your mother insists upon your accepting

157
00:14:39,299 --> 00:14:44,032
it. Is it not so, Mrs. Bennet?” “Yes, or I will never see her again.”

158
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:49,132
“An unhappy alternative is before you, Elizabeth. From this day you

159
00:14:49,132 --> 00:14:52,799
must be a stranger to one of your parents. Your mother will never see

160
00:14:52,799 --> 00:14:57,233
you again if you do not marry Mr. Collins, and I will never see you again if you do.”

161
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:01,500
Elizabeth could not but smile at such a conclusion of such a beginning;

162
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:06,233
but Mrs. Bennet, who had persuaded herself that her husband regarded the affair as she wished,

163
00:15:06,233 --> 00:15:09,765
was excessively disappointed. “What do you mean,

164
00:15:09,765 --> 00:15:14,566
Mr. Bennet, by talking in this way? You promised me to insist upon her marrying him.”

165
00:15:15,233 --> 00:15:21,100
“My dear,” replied her husband, “I have two small favours to request. First,

166
00:15:21,100 --> 00:15:24,466
that you will allow me the free use of my understanding on the present occasion;

167
00:15:24,466 --> 00:15:30,333
and, secondly, of my room. I shall be glad to have the library to myself as soon as may be.”

168
00:15:30,333 --> 00:15:35,333
Not yet, however, in spite of her disappointment in her husband, did Mrs. Bennet give up the point.

169
00:15:35,333 --> 00:15:41,765
She talked to Elizabeth again and again; coaxed and threatened her by turns. She endeavoured to

170
00:15:41,765 --> 00:15:47,032
secure Jane in her interest, but Jane, with all possible mildness, declined interfering;

171
00:15:47,032 --> 00:15:52,600
and Elizabeth, sometimes with real earnestness, and sometimes with playful gaiety, replied to

172
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:57,299
her attacks. Though her manner varied, however, her determination never did.

173
00:15:58,200 --> 00:16:03,100
Mr. Collins, meanwhile, was meditating in solitude on what had passed. He thought too

174
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:06,665
well of himself to comprehend on what motive his cousin could refuse him;

175
00:16:06,665 --> 00:16:12,466
and though his pride was hurt, he suffered in no other way. His regard for her was quite imaginary;

176
00:16:12,466 --> 00:16:16,899
and the possibility of her deserving her mother’s reproach prevented his feeling any regret.

177
00:16:16,899 --> 00:16:22,500
While the family were in this confusion, Charlotte Lucas came to spend the day with them. She was met

178
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:28,132
in the vestibule by Lydia, who, flying to her, cried in a half whisper, “I am glad you are come,

179
00:16:28,132 --> 00:16:33,966
for there is such fun here! What do you think has happened this morning? Mr. Collins has made an

180
00:16:33,966 --> 00:16:38,933
offer to Lizzy, and she will not have him.” Charlotte had hardly time to answer

181
00:16:38,933 --> 00:16:44,100
before they were joined by Kitty, who came to tell the same news; and no sooner had they entered the

182
00:16:44,100 --> 00:16:49,000
breakfast-room, where Mrs. Bennet was alone, than she likewise began on the subject, calling

183
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:53,700
on Miss Lucas for her compassion, and entreating her to persuade her friend Lizzy to comply with

184
00:16:53,700 --> 00:17:00,100
the wishes of her family. “Pray do, my dear Miss Lucas,” she added, in a melancholy tone; “for

185
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:07,265
nobody is on my side, nobody takes part with me; I am cruelly used, nobody feels for my poor nerves.”

186
00:17:07,865 --> 00:17:11,265
Charlotte’s reply was spared by the entrance of Jane and Elizabeth.

187
00:17:12,066 --> 00:17:19,299
“Ay, there she comes,” continued Mrs. Bennet, “looking as unconcerned as may be, and caring no

188
00:17:19,299 --> 00:17:24,899
more for us than if we were at York, provided she can have her own way. But I tell you what, Miss

189
00:17:24,899 --> 00:17:29,799
Lizzy, if you take it into your head to go on refusing every offer of marriage in this way, you

190
00:17:29,799 --> 00:17:34,132
will never get a husband at all—and I am sure I do not know who is to maintain you when your father

191
00:17:34,132 --> 00:17:40,000
is dead. I shall not be able to keep you—and so I warn you. I have done with you from this very day.

192
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:44,165
I told you in the library, you know, that I should never speak to you again, and you will find me as

193
00:17:44,165 --> 00:17:49,500
good as my word. I have no pleasure in talking to undutiful children. Not that I have much pleasure,

194
00:17:49,500 --> 00:17:53,500
indeed, in talking to anybody. People who suffer as I do from nervous complaints can

195
00:17:53,500 --> 00:17:58,899
have no great inclination for talking. Nobody can tell what I suffer! But it is always so.

196
00:17:58,899 --> 00:18:03,966
Those who do not complain are never pitied.” Her daughters listened in silence to this

197
00:18:03,966 --> 00:18:07,566
effusion, sensible that any attempt to reason with or soothe her would

198
00:18:07,566 --> 00:18:11,799
only increase the irritation. She talked on, therefore, without interruption from any of

199
00:18:11,799 --> 00:18:16,433
them till they were joined by Mr. Collins, who entered with an air more stately than usual,

200
00:18:16,433 --> 00:18:22,600
and on perceiving whom, she said to the girls,— “Now, I do insist upon it, that you, all of you,

201
00:18:22,600 --> 00:18:26,200
hold your tongues, and let Mr. Collins and me have a little conversation together.”

202
00:18:26,933 --> 00:18:32,399
Elizabeth passed quietly out of the room, Jane and Kitty followed, but Lydia stood her ground,

203
00:18:32,399 --> 00:18:37,665
determined to hear all she could; and Charlotte, detained first by the civility of Mr. Collins,

204
00:18:37,665 --> 00:18:42,899
whose inquiries after herself and all her family were very minute, and then by a little curiosity,

205
00:18:42,899 --> 00:18:48,600
satisfied herself with walking to the window and pretending not to hear. In a doleful voice Mrs.

206
00:18:48,600 --> 00:18:52,733
Bennet thus began the projected conversation:— “Oh, Mr. Collins!”

207
00:18:53,632 --> 00:19:00,532
“My dear madam,” replied he, “let us be for ever silent on this point. Far be it from me,”

208
00:19:00,532 --> 00:19:06,233
he presently continued, in a voice that marked his displeasure, “to resent the behaviour of

209
00:19:06,233 --> 00:19:12,165
your daughter. Resignation to inevitable evils is the duty of us all: the peculiar duty of a

210
00:19:12,165 --> 00:19:16,966
young man who has been so fortunate as I have been, in early preferment; and, I trust, I am

211
00:19:16,966 --> 00:19:22,600
resigned. Perhaps not the less so from feeling a doubt of my positive happiness had my fair

212
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:27,833
cousin honoured me with her hand; for I have often observed, that resignation is never so perfect as

213
00:19:27,833 --> 00:19:33,299
when the blessing denied begins to lose somewhat of its value in our estimation. You will not,

214
00:19:33,299 --> 00:19:38,865
I hope, consider me as showing any disrespect to your family, my dear madam, by thus withdrawing

215
00:19:38,865 --> 00:19:43,000
my pretensions to your daughter’s favour, without having paid yourself and Mr. Bennet

216
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:48,233
the compliment of requesting you to interpose your authority in my behalf. My conduct may, I fear,

217
00:19:48,233 --> 00:19:53,566
be objectionable in having accepted my dismission from your daughter’s lips instead of your own;

218
00:19:53,566 --> 00:19:59,700
but we are all liable to error. I have certainly meant well through the whole affair. My object has

219
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:04,966
been to secure an amiable companion for myself, with due consideration for the advantage of all

220
00:20:04,966 --> 00:20:11,165
your family; and if my manner has been at all reprehensible, I here beg leave to apologize.”

221
00:20:13,733 --> 00:20:14,700
CHAPTER XXI.

222
00:20:21,966 --> 00:20:27,066
THE discussion of Mr. Collins’s offer was now nearly at an end, and Elizabeth had only to

223
00:20:27,066 --> 00:20:32,200
suffer from the uncomfortable feelings necessarily attending it, and occasionally from some peevish

224
00:20:32,200 --> 00:20:37,899
allusion of her mother. As for the gentleman himself, his feelings were chiefly expressed,

225
00:20:37,899 --> 00:20:44,466
not by embarrassment or dejection, or by trying to avoid her, but by stiffness of manner and

226
00:20:44,466 --> 00:20:50,799
resentful silence. He scarcely ever spoke to her; and the assiduous attentions which he had been so

227
00:20:50,799 --> 00:20:55,865
sensible of himself were transferred for the rest of the day to Miss Lucas, whose civility

228
00:20:55,865 --> 00:21:00,532
in listening to him was a seasonable relief to them all, and especially to her friend.

229
00:21:01,299 --> 00:21:07,433
The morrow produced no abatement of Mrs. Bennet’s ill humour or ill health. Mr. Collins was also

230
00:21:07,433 --> 00:21:12,899
in the same state of angry pride. Elizabeth had hoped that his resentment might shorten his visit,

231
00:21:12,899 --> 00:21:18,665
but his plan did not appear in the least affected by it. He was always to have gone on Saturday,

232
00:21:18,665 --> 00:21:24,966
and to Saturday he still meant to stay. After breakfast, the girls walked to Meryton,

233
00:21:24,966 --> 00:21:29,399
to inquire if Mr. Wickham were returned, and to lament over his absence from the Netherfield

234
00:21:29,399 --> 00:21:33,966
ball. He joined them on their entering the town, and attended them to their aunt’s,

235
00:21:33,966 --> 00:21:39,665
where his regret and vexation and the concern of everybody were well talked over. To Elizabeth,

236
00:21:39,665 --> 00:21:44,632
however, he voluntarily acknowledged that the necessity of his absence had been self-imposed.

237
00:21:44,632 --> 00:21:50,399
“I found,” said he, “as the time drew near, that I had better not meet Mr.

238
00:21:50,399 --> 00:21:55,665
Darcy;—that to be in the same room, the same party with him for so many hours together,

239
00:21:55,665 --> 00:22:01,566
might be more than I could bear, and that scenes might arise unpleasant to more than myself.”

240
00:22:02,700 --> 00:22:07,299
She highly approved his forbearance; and they had leisure for a full discussion of it,

241
00:22:07,299 --> 00:22:12,233
and for all the commendations which they civilly bestowed on each other, as Wickham and another

242
00:22:12,233 --> 00:22:17,200
officer walked back with them to Longbourn, and during the walk he particularly attended to her.

243
00:22:17,200 --> 00:22:22,566
His accompanying them was a double advantage: she felt all the compliment it offered to herself;

244
00:22:22,566 --> 00:22:26,566
and it was most acceptable as an occasion of introducing him to her father and mother.

245
00:22:29,933 --> 00:22:34,000
to Miss Bennet; it came from Netherfield, and was opened immediately. The envelope contained

246
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:39,000
a sheet of elegant, little, hot-pressed paper, well covered with a lady’s fair, flowing hand;

247
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:44,865
and Elizabeth saw her sister’s countenance change as she read it, and saw her dwelling intently on

248
00:22:44,865 --> 00:22:51,165
some particular passages. Jane recollected herself soon; and putting the letter away, tried to join,

249
00:22:51,165 --> 00:22:55,899
with her usual cheerfulness, in the general conversation: but Elizabeth felt an anxiety on

250
00:22:55,899 --> 00:23:00,700
the subject which drew off her attention even from Wickham; and no sooner had he and his companion

251
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:05,933
taken leave, than a glance from Jane invited her to follow her upstairs. When they had gained

252
00:23:05,933 --> 00:23:11,865
their own room, Jane, taking out her letter, said, “This is from Caroline Bingley: what it

253
00:23:11,865 --> 00:23:17,566
contains has surprised me a good deal. The whole party have left Netherfield by this time, and are

254
00:23:17,566 --> 00:23:23,632
on their way to town; and without any intention of coming back again. You shall hear what she says.”

255
00:23:23,632 --> 00:23:28,733
She then read the first sentence aloud, which comprised the information of their having just

256
00:23:28,733 --> 00:23:33,233
resolved to follow their brother to town directly, and of their meaning to dine that day in Grosvenor

257
00:23:33,233 --> 00:23:39,700
Street, where Mr. Hurst had a house. The next was in these words:—“‘I do not pretend to regret

258
00:23:39,700 --> 00:23:45,833
anything I shall leave in Hertfordshire except your society, my dearest friend; but we will hope,

259
00:23:45,833 --> 00:23:51,165
at some future period, to enjoy many returns of that delightful intercourse we have known,

260
00:23:51,165 --> 00:23:56,765
and in the meanwhile may lessen the pain of separation by a very frequent and most unreserved

261
00:23:56,765 --> 00:24:03,165
correspondence. I depend on you for that.’” To these high-flown expressions Elizabeth listened

262
00:24:03,165 --> 00:24:07,899
with all the insensibility of distrust; and though the suddenness of their removal surprised her,

263
00:24:07,899 --> 00:24:12,466
she saw nothing in it really to lament: it was not to be supposed that their absence from

264
00:24:12,466 --> 00:24:16,500
Netherfield would prevent Mr. Bingley’s being there; and as to the loss of their society,

265
00:24:16,500 --> 00:24:21,299
she was persuaded that Jane must soon cease to regard it in the enjoyment of his.

266
00:24:22,265 --> 00:24:27,600
“It is unlucky,” said she, after a short pause, “that you should not be able to see your friends

267
00:24:27,600 --> 00:24:32,100
before they leave the country. But may we not hope that the period of future happiness,

268
00:24:32,100 --> 00:24:35,799
to which Miss Bingley looks forward, may arrive earlier than she is aware,

269
00:24:35,799 --> 00:24:39,665
and that the delightful intercourse you have known as friends will be renewed with yet

270
00:24:39,665 --> 00:24:45,100
greater satisfaction as sisters? Mr. Bingley will not be detained in London by them.”

271
00:24:45,899 --> 00:24:49,500
“Caroline decidedly says that none of the party will return into Hertfordshire

272
00:24:49,500 --> 00:24:53,700
this winter. I will read it to you. “‘When my brother left us yesterday,

273
00:24:53,700 --> 00:24:58,733
he imagined that the business which took him to London might be concluded in three or four days;

274
00:24:58,733 --> 00:25:04,733
but as we are certain it cannot be so, and at the same time convinced that when Charles gets to

275
00:25:04,733 --> 00:25:08,933
town he will be in no hurry to leave it again, we have determined on following him thither,

276
00:25:08,933 --> 00:25:14,466
that he may not be obliged to spend his vacant hours in a comfortless hotel. Many of

277
00:25:14,466 --> 00:25:19,799
my acquaintance are already there for the winter: I wish I could hear that you, my dearest friend,

278
00:25:19,799 --> 00:25:25,665
had any intention of making one in the crowd, but of that I despair. I sincerely hope your Christmas

279
00:25:25,665 --> 00:25:30,665
in Hertfordshire may abound in the gaieties which that season generally brings, and that your beaux

280
00:25:30,665 --> 00:25:35,500
will be so numerous as to prevent your feeling the loss of the three of whom we shall deprive you.’

281
00:25:36,365 --> 00:25:41,466
“It is evident by this,” added Jane, “that he comes back no more this winter.”

282
00:25:42,233 --> 00:25:45,765
“It is only evident that Miss Bingley does not mean he should.”

283
00:25:46,433 --> 00:25:54,100
“Why will you think so? It must be his own doing; he is his own master. But you do not know all. I

284
00:25:54,100 --> 00:25:58,665
will read you the passage which particularly hurts me. I will have no reserves from you.

285
00:25:59,632 --> 00:26:06,399
‘Mr. Darcy is impatient to see his sister; and to confess the truth, we are scarcely

286
00:26:06,399 --> 00:26:11,799
less eager to meet her again. I really do not think Georgiana Darcy has her equal for beauty,

287
00:26:11,799 --> 00:26:17,200
elegance, and accomplishments; and the affection she inspires in Louisa and myself is heightened

288
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:22,433
into something still more interesting from the hope we dare to entertain of her being hereafter

289
00:26:22,433 --> 00:26:28,566
our sister. I do not know whether I ever before mentioned to you my feelings on this subject,

290
00:26:28,566 --> 00:26:32,566
but I will not leave the country without confiding them, and I trust you will not

291
00:26:32,566 --> 00:26:38,566
esteem them unreasonable. My brother admires her greatly already; he will have frequent opportunity

292
00:26:38,566 --> 00:26:43,265
now of seeing her on the most intimate footing; her relations all wish the connection as much

293
00:26:43,265 --> 00:26:48,399
as his own; and a sister’s partiality is not misleading me, I think, when I call Charles

294
00:26:48,399 --> 00:26:54,532
most capable of engaging any woman’s heart. With all these circumstances to favour an attachment,

295
00:26:54,532 --> 00:27:00,532
and nothing to prevent it, am I wrong, my dearest Jane, in indulging the hope of an

296
00:27:00,532 --> 00:27:04,966
event which will secure the happiness of so many?’ What think you of this sentence,

297
00:27:04,966 --> 00:27:11,200
my dear Lizzy?” said Jane, as she finished it. “Is it not clear enough? Does it not expressly declare

298
00:27:11,200 --> 00:27:16,066
that Caroline neither expects nor wishes me to be her sister; that she is perfectly convinced

299
00:27:16,066 --> 00:27:20,700
of her brother’s indifference; and that if she suspects the nature of my feelings for him she

300
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:25,365
means (most kindly!) to put me on my guard. Can there be any other opinion on the subject?”

301
00:27:26,100 --> 00:27:30,233
“Yes, there can; for mine is totally different. Will you hear it?”

302
00:27:30,765 --> 00:27:35,265
“Most willingly.” “You shall have it in a few words. Miss Bingley

303
00:27:35,265 --> 00:27:40,000
sees that her brother is in love with you and wants him to marry Miss Darcy. She follows him to

304
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:44,333
town in the hope of keeping him there, and tries to persuade you that he does not care about you.”

305
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:49,265
Jane shook her head. “Indeed, Jane, you ought to believe me.

306
00:27:49,265 --> 00:27:54,665
No one who has ever seen you together can doubt his affection; Miss Bingley, I am sure, cannot:

307
00:27:54,665 --> 00:28:00,233
she is not such a simpleton. Could she have seen half as much love in Mr. Darcy for herself,

308
00:28:00,233 --> 00:28:05,500
she would have ordered her wedding clothes. But the case is this:—we are not rich enough or grand

309
00:28:05,500 --> 00:28:10,299
enough for them; and she is the more anxious to get Miss Darcy for her brother, from the notion

310
00:28:10,299 --> 00:28:14,566
that when there has been one inter-marriage, she may have less trouble in achieving a second;

311
00:28:14,566 --> 00:28:21,066
in which there is certainly some ingenuity, and I dare say it would succeed if Miss de Bourgh were

312
00:28:21,066 --> 00:28:26,466
out of the way. But, my dearest Jane, you cannot seriously imagine that, because Miss Bingley tells

313
00:28:26,466 --> 00:28:31,365
you her brother greatly admires Miss Darcy, he is in the smallest degree less sensible of your merit

314
00:28:31,365 --> 00:28:36,233
than when he took leave of you on Tuesday; or that it will be in her power to persuade him that,

315
00:28:36,233 --> 00:28:39,399
instead of being in love with you, he is very much in love with her friend.”

316
00:28:39,399 --> 00:28:44,933
“If we thought alike of Miss Bingley,” replied Jane, “your representation of all this might

317
00:28:44,933 --> 00:28:50,799
make me quite easy. But I know the foundation is unjust. Caroline is incapable of wilfully

318
00:28:50,799 --> 00:28:55,733
deceiving anyone; and all that I can hope in this case is, that she is deceived herself.”

319
00:28:56,632 --> 00:29:00,000
“That is right. You could not have started a more happy idea,

320
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:03,899
since you will not take comfort in mine: believe her to be deceived,

321
00:29:03,899 --> 00:29:08,765
by all means. You have now done your duty by her, and must fret no longer.”

322
00:29:09,532 --> 00:29:13,265
“But, my dear sister, can I be happy, even supposing the best,

323
00:29:13,265 --> 00:29:17,399
in accepting a man whose sisters and friends are all wishing him to marry elsewhere?”

324
00:29:18,233 --> 00:29:24,100
“You must decide for yourself,” said Elizabeth; “and if, upon mature deliberation, you find that

325
00:29:24,100 --> 00:29:28,299
the misery of disobliging his two sisters is more than equivalent to the happiness of being his

326
00:29:28,299 --> 00:29:35,500
wife, I advise you, by all means, to refuse him.” “How can you talk so?” said Jane, faintly smiling;

327
00:29:35,500 --> 00:29:39,966
“you must know, that, though I should be exceedingly grieved at their disapprobation,

328
00:29:39,966 --> 00:29:43,600
I could not hesitate.” “I did not think you would;

329
00:29:43,600 --> 00:29:48,299
and that being the case, I cannot consider your situation with much compassion.”

330
00:29:49,066 --> 00:29:53,933
“But if he returns no more this winter, my choice will never be required. A thousand

331
00:29:53,933 --> 00:29:58,365
things may arise in six months.” The idea of his returning no more

332
00:29:58,365 --> 00:30:02,865
Elizabeth treated with the utmost contempt. It appeared to her merely the suggestion of

333
00:30:02,865 --> 00:30:07,000
Caroline’s interested wishes; and she could not for a moment suppose that those wishes,

334
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:12,466
however openly or artfully spoken, could influence a young man so totally independent of everyone.

335
00:30:13,365 --> 00:30:17,265
She represented to her sister, as forcibly as possible, what she felt on the subject,

336
00:30:17,265 --> 00:30:22,433
and had soon the pleasure of seeing its happy effect. Jane’s temper was not desponding;

337
00:30:22,433 --> 00:30:26,466
and she was gradually led to hope, though the diffidence of affection sometimes

338
00:30:26,466 --> 00:30:31,200
overcame the hope, that Bingley would return to Netherfield, and answer every wish of her heart.

339
00:30:31,799 --> 00:30:36,399
They agreed that Mrs. Bennet should only hear of the departure of the family, without being alarmed

340
00:30:36,399 --> 00:30:41,032
on the score of the gentleman’s conduct; but even this partial communication gave her a great deal

341
00:30:41,032 --> 00:30:45,233
of concern, and she bewailed it as exceedingly unlucky that the ladies should happen to go

342
00:30:45,233 --> 00:30:49,799
away just as they were all getting so intimate together. After lamenting it, however, at some

343
00:30:49,799 --> 00:30:53,799
length, she had the consolation of thinking that Mr. Bingley would be soon down again, and soon

344
00:30:53,799 --> 00:30:58,600
dining at Longbourn; and the conclusion of all was the comfortable declaration, that, though

345
00:30:58,600 --> 00:31:03,532
he had been invited only to a family dinner, she would take care to have two full courses.

346
00:31:05,865 --> 00:31:06,966
CHAPTER XXII.
